.SH "1\&.16\&.1"
.PP
.SS "Patch Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Support \fRunstable_dataStrategy\fP on \fRstaticHandler\&.queryRoute\fP (\fR#11515\fP)
.PP
.SH "1\&.16\&.0"
.PP
.SS "Minor Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Add a new \fRunstable_dataStrategy\fP configuration option (\fR#11098\fP)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
This option allows Data Router applications to take control over the approach for executing route loaders and actions
.IP "  \(bu" 4
The default implementation is today's behavior, to fetch all loaders in parallel, but this option allows users to implement more advanced data flows including Remix single-fetch, middleware/context APIs, automatic loader caching, and more
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Move \fRunstable_dataStrategy\fP from \fRcreateStaticHandler\fP to \fRstaticHandler\&.query\fP so it can be request-specific for use with the \fRResponseStub\fP approach in Remix\&. It's not really applicable to \fRqueryRoute\fP for now since that's a singular handler call anyway so any pre-processing/post/processing could be done there manually\&. (\fR#11377\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Add a new \fRfuture\&.unstable_skipActionRevalidation\fP future flag (\fR#11098\fP)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Currently, active loaders revalidate after any action, regardless of the result
.IP "  \(bu" 4
With this flag enabled, actions that return/throw a 4xx/5xx response status will no longer automatically revalidate
.IP "  \(bu" 4
This should reduce load on your server since it's rare that a 4xx/5xx should actually mutate any data
.IP "  \(bu" 4
If you need to revalidate after a 4xx/5xx result with this flag enabled, you can still do that via returning \fRtrue\fP from \fRshouldRevalidate\fP
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRshouldRevalidate\fP now also receives a new \fRunstable_actionStatus\fP argument alongside \fRactionResult\fP so you can make decision based on the status of the \fRaction\fP response without having to encode it into the action data
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
Added a \fRskipLoaderErrorBubbling\fP flag to \fRstaticHandler\&.query\fP to disable error bubbling on loader executions for single-fetch scenarios where the client-side router will handle the bubbling (\fR#11098\fP)
.PP
.SH "1\&.15\&.3"
.PP
.SS "Patch Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Fix a \fRfuture\&.v7_partialHydration\fP bug that would re-run loaders below the boundary on hydration if SSR loader errors bubbled to a parent boundary (\fR#11324\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Fix a \fRfuture\&.v7_partialHydration\fP bug that would consider the router uninitialized if a route did not have a loader (\fR#11325\fP)
.PP
.SH "1\&.15\&.2"
.PP
.SS "Patch Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Preserve hydrated errors during partial hydration runs (\fR#11305\fP)
.PP
.SH "1\&.15\&.1"
.PP
.SS "Patch Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Fix encoding/decoding issues with pre-encoded dynamic parameter values (\fR#11199\fP)
.PP
.SH "1\&.15\&.0"
.PP
.SS "Minor Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Add a \fRcreateStaticHandler\fP \fRfuture\&.v7_throwAbortReason\fP flag to throw \fRrequest\&.signal\&.reason\fP (defaults to a \fRDOMException\fP) when a request is aborted instead of an \fRError\fP such as \fRnew Error("query() call aborted: GET /path")\fP (\fR#11104\fP)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Please note that \fRDOMException\fP was added in Node v17 so you will not get a \fRDOMException\fP on Node 16 and below\&.
.PP

.PP
.SS "Patch Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Respect the \fRErrorResponse\fP status code if passed to \fRgetStaticContextFormError\fP (\fR#11213\fP)
.PP
.SH "1\&.14\&.2"
.PP
.SS "Patch Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Fix bug where dashes were not picked up in dynamic parameter names (\fR#11160\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Do not attempt to deserialize empty JSON responses (\fR#11164\fP)
.PP
.SH "1\&.14\&.1"
.PP
.SS "Patch Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Fix bug with \fRroute\&.lazy\fP not working correctly on initial SPA load when \fRv7_partialHydration\fP is specified (\fR#11121\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Fix bug preventing revalidation from occurring for persisted fetchers unmounted during the \fRsubmitting\fP phase (\fR#11102\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
De-dup relative path logic in \fRresolveTo\fP (\fR#11097\fP)
.PP
.SH "1\&.14\&.0"
.PP
.SS "Minor Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Added a new \fRfuture\&.v7_partialHydration\fP future flag that enables partial hydration of a data router when Server-Side Rendering\&. This allows you to provide \fRhydrationData\&.loaderData\fP that has values for \fIsome\fP initially matched route loaders, but not all\&. When this flag is enabled, the router will call \fRloader\fP functions for routes that do not have hydration loader data during \fRrouter\&.initialize()\fP, and it will render down to the deepest provided \fRHydrateFallback\fP (up to the first route without hydration data) while it executes the unhydrated routes\&. (\fR#11033\fP)

.PP
For example, the following router has a \fRroot\fP and \fRindex\fP route, but only provided \fRhydrationData\&.loaderData\fP for the \fRroot\fP route\&. Because the \fRindex\fP route has a \fRloader\fP, we need to run that during initialization\&. With \fRfuture\&.v7_partialHydration\fP specified, \fR<RouterProvider>\fP will render the \fRRootComponent\fP (because it has data) and then the \fRIndexFallback\fP (since it does not have data)\&. Once \fRindexLoader\fP finishes, application will update and display \fRIndexComponent\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
let router = createBrowserRouter(
  [
    {
      id: "root",
      path: "/",
      loader: rootLoader,
      Component: RootComponent,
      Fallback: RootFallback,
      children: [
        {
          id: "index",
          index: true,
          loader: indexLoader,
          Component: IndexComponent,
          HydrateFallback: IndexFallback,
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
  {
    future: {
      v7_partialHydration: true,
    },
    hydrationData: {
      loaderData: {
        root: { message: "Hydrated from Root!" },
      },
    },
  }
);
.fi
.PP

.PP
If the above example did not have an \fRIndexFallback\fP, then \fRRouterProvider\fP would instead render the \fRRootFallback\fP while it executed the \fRindexLoader\fP\&.

.PP
\fBNote:\fP When \fRfuture\&.v7_partialHydration\fP is provided, the \fR<RouterProvider fallbackElement>\fP prop is ignored since you can move it to a \fRFallback\fP on your top-most route\&. The \fRfallbackElement\fP prop will be removed in React Router v7 when \fRv7_partialHydration\fP behavior becomes the standard behavior\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Add a new \fRfuture\&.v7_relativeSplatPath\fP flag to implement a breaking bug fix to relative routing when inside a splat route\&. (\fR#11087\fP)

.PP
This fix was originally added in \fR#10983\fP and was later reverted in \fR#11078\fP because it was determined that a large number of existing applications were relying on the buggy behavior (see \fR#11052\fP)

.PP
\fBThe Bug\fP The buggy behavior is that without this flag, the default behavior when resolving relative paths is to \fIignore\fP any splat (\fR*\fP) portion of the current route path\&.

.PP
\fBThe Background\fP This decision was originally made thinking that it would make the concept of nested different sections of your apps in \fR<Routes>\fP easier if relative routing would \fIreplace\fP the current splat:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<BrowserRouter>
  <Routes>
    <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
    <Route path="dashboard/*" element={<Dashboard />} />
  </Routes>
</BrowserRouter>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Any paths like \fR/dashboard\fP, \fR/dashboard/team\fP, \fR/dashboard/projects\fP will match the \fRDashboard\fP route\&. The dashboard component itself can then render nested \fR<Routes>\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
function Dashboard() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Dashboard</h2>
      <nav>
        <Link to="/">Dashboard Home</Link>
        <Link to="team">Team</Link>
        <Link to="projects">Projects</Link>
      </nav>

      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<DashboardHome />} />
        <Route path="team" element={<DashboardTeam />} />
        <Route path="projects" element={<DashboardProjects />} />
      </Routes>
    </div>
  );
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
Now, all links and route paths are relative to the router above them\&. This makes code splitting and compartmentalizing your app really easy\&. You could render the \fRDashboard\fP as its own independent app, or embed it into your large app without making any changes to it\&.

.PP
\fBThe Problem\fP

.PP
The problem is that this concept of ignoring part of a path breaks a lot of other assumptions in React Router - namely that \fR"\&."\fP always means the current location pathname for that route\&. When we ignore the splat portion, we start getting invalid paths when using \fR"\&."\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// If we are on URL /dashboard/team, and we want to link to /dashboard/team:
function DashboardTeam() {
  // ❌ This is broken and results in <a href="/dashboard">
  return <Link to="\&.">A broken link to the Current URL</Link>;

  // ✅ This is fixed but super unintuitive since we're already at /dashboard/team!
  return <Link to="\&./team">A broken link to the Current URL</Link>;
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
We've also introduced an issue that we can no longer move our \fRDashboardTeam\fP component around our route hierarchy easily - since it behaves differently if we're underneath a non-splat route, such as \fR/dashboard/:widget\fP\&. Now, our \fR"\&."\fP links will, properly point to ourself \fIinclusive of the dynamic param value\fP so behavior will break from it's corresponding usage in a \fR/dashboard/*\fP route\&.

.PP
Even worse, consider a nested splat route configuration:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<BrowserRouter>
  <Routes>
    <Route path="dashboard">
      <Route path="*" element={<Dashboard />} />
    </Route>
  </Routes>
</BrowserRouter>
.fi
.PP

.PP
Now, a \fR<Link to="\&.">\fP and a \fR<Link to="\&.\&.">\fP inside the \fRDashboard\fP component go to the same place! That is definitely not correct!

.PP
Another common issue arose in Data Routers (and Remix) where any \fR<Form>\fP should post to it's own route \fRaction\fP if you the user doesn't specify a form action:

.PP
.PP
.nf
let router = createBrowserRouter({
  path: "/dashboard",
  children: [
    {
      path: "*",
      action: dashboardAction,
      Component() {
        // ❌ This form is broken!  It throws a 405 error when it submits because
        // it tries to submit to /dashboard (without the splat value) and the parent
        // `/dashboard` route doesn't have an action
        return <Form method="post">\&.\&.\&.</Form>;
      },
    },
  ],
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
This is just a compounded issue from the above because the default location for a \fRForm\fP to submit to is itself (\fR"\&."\fP) - and if we ignore the splat portion, that now resolves to the parent route\&.

.PP
\fBThe Solution\fP If you are leveraging this behavior, it's recommended to enable the future flag, move your splat to it's own route, and leverage \fR\&.\&./\fP for any links to "sibling" pages:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<BrowserRouter>
  <Routes>
    <Route path="dashboard">
      <Route index path="*" element={<Dashboard />} />
    </Route>
  </Routes>
</BrowserRouter>

function Dashboard() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Dashboard</h2>
      <nav>
        <Link to="\&.\&.">Dashboard Home</Link>
        <Link to="\&.\&./team">Team</Link>
        <Link to="\&.\&./projects">Projects</Link>
      </nav>

      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<DashboardHome />} />
        <Route path="team" element={<DashboardTeam />} />
        <Route path="projects" element={<DashboardProjects />} />
      </Router>
    </div>
  );
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
This way, \fR\&.\fP means "the full current pathname for my route" in all cases (including static, dynamic, and splat routes) and \fR\&.\&.\fP always means "my parents pathname"\&.
.PP
.SS "Patch Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Catch and bubble errors thrown when trying to unwrap responses from \fRloader\fP/\fRaction\fP functions (\fR#11061\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
