.SS "Minor Changes"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBIntroducing Lazy Route Modules!\fP (\fR#10045\fP)

.PP
In order to keep your application bundles small and support code-splitting of your routes, we've introduced a new \fRlazy()\fP route property\&. This is an async function that resolves the non-route-matching portions of your route definition (\fRloader\fP, \fRaction\fP, \fRelement\fP/\fRComponent\fP, \fRerrorElement\fP/\fRErrorBoundary\fP, \fRshouldRevalidate\fP, \fRhandle\fP)\&.

.PP
Lazy routes are resolved on initial load and during the \fRloading\fP or \fRsubmitting\fP phase of a navigation or fetcher call\&. You cannot lazily define route-matching properties (\fRpath\fP, \fRindex\fP, \fRchildren\fP) since we only execute your lazy route functions after we've matched known routes\&.

.PP
Your \fRlazy\fP functions will typically return the result of a dynamic import\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// In this example, we assume most folks land on the homepage so we include that
// in our critical\-path bundle, but then we lazily load modules for /a and /b so
// they don't load until the user navigates to those routes
let routes = createRoutesFromElements(
  <Route path="/" element={<Layout />}>
    <Route index element={<Home />} />
    <Route path="a" lazy={() => import("\&./a")} />
    <Route path="b" lazy={() => import("\&./b")} />
  </Route>
);
.fi
.PP

.PP
Then in your lazy route modules, export the properties you want defined for the route:

.PP
.PP
.nf
export async function loader({ request }) {
  let data = await fetchData(request);
  return json(data);
}

// Export a `Component` directly instead of needing to create a React Element from it
export function Component() {
  let data = useLoaderData();

  return (
    <>
      <h1>You made it!</h1>
      <p>{data}</p>
    </>
  );
}

// Export an `ErrorBoundary` directly instead of needing to create a React Element from it
export function ErrorBoundary() {
  let error = useRouteError();
  return isRouteErrorResponse(error) ? (
    <h1>
      {error\&.status} {error\&.statusText}
    </h1>
  ) : (
    <h1>{error\&.message || error}</h1>
  );
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
An example of this in action can be found in the \fR\fRexamples/lazy-loading-router-provider\fP\fP directory of the repository\&.

